{
  "file": "crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[node_content]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This storage-only consumer must delegate load/store using node_path\n without resolving contents. Graph requires the callback, but invoking\n this guard would violate the no-content-read invariant of the test.\n Other graph fixtures exercise content resolution."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[node_content]` is a valid coverage exception.\n\n- **Form:** It is a fenced `{cite}` with commentary on an explicit function that has a body. The built-in allows this form.\n- **Reason:** The commentary gives a substantive reason, not just difficulty. `StoredGraph` is a storage-only fixture. `Graph` requires the `node_content` callback. The test `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` asserts that load and store delegate through `node_path` alone. So the body is a `panic!` guard, and running it would break the no-content-read invariant.\n- **Agreement with source:** `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` in `impl[StoredGraph/EvidenceStorage]` call only `self.node_path(node)` and the underlying `FileEvidenceStorage`. They never call `node_content`, so the guard is correctly left unexecuted.\n- **Other fixtures:** The claim that other fixtures exercise content resolution holds. `Memory` in `tests/agent.rs`, `Memory` in `tests/validation.rs` and the `graph` macro in `tests/contracts.rs` all give `node_content` real behavior.\n\nI found no gap.",
